File Index

/index.ts
Purpose: Package entry — re-exports the device-credentials surface (DeviceCredentials, NeedsEnrollmentError, the storage adapters, deviceAssertionPayload) alongside the original fetchJsonClient helper.

/device-credentials.ts
Purpose: Browser half of the device-bound credential scheme (server half: apps/auth-server/src/routes/device.ts). Generates a NON-EXTRACTABLE ECDSA P-256 keypair (`generateKey(..., extractable=false, ...)` — the load-bearing argument: exportKey on the private half throws forever, so page-level code cannot steal the key), persists it as a CryptoKey in IndexedDB (which is WHY IndexedDB and not localStorage — localStorage holds only strings, so it would force an extractable key), enrolls only the public JWK, and trades signatures for short-lived per-service access tokens held in a Map in MEMORY. Nothing is ever written to localStorage/sessionStorage, and there is a test asserting exactly that. `subtle`, `storage` and `fetchImpl` are injectable: jsdom and React Native both lack WebCrypto and IndexedDB, so the seam that makes this testable is the same one that will make it reusable in shatteredarchive-mobile. deviceAssertionPayload MUST stay byte-identical to the server's (length-prefixed, not delimiter-joined) — drift there silently breaks every enrolled device in the field. Tokens are cached PER SERVICE because they are audience-scoped, re-minted a refresh-skew before expiry so one cannot lapse mid-flight, and concurrent mints are coalesced via an in-flight map (without it a page load firing a dozen requests would run a dozen challenge/assert round trips and burn a dozen nonces). A DEVICE_REENROLL_REQUIRED response clears the stale deviceId and throws the typed NeedsEnrollmentError so the UI prompts a sign-in instead of looping; any other assert failure keeps the enrollment intact. authedFetch retries a 401 exactly ONCE with a fresh token, then returns the 401 rather than looping.

/__tests__/device-credentials.test.ts
Purpose: Runs under the NODE environment (see jest.config.cjs) against REAL WebCrypto, and the fake auth-server actually VERIFIES each signature with the enrolled public key — a fake that merely returned a token would let a payload-format or signature-encoding bug through, and those are exactly the bugs that only surface against a real browser. Pins: private key is non-extractable while the public half still exports, enrollment sends no `d`, per-service token separation, cache hit, refresh-skew re-mint, concurrent-mint coalescing into ONE round trip, NeedsEnrollmentError when never enrolled and on DEVICE_REENROLL_REQUIRED (with the stale id cleared), a non-re-enrollment failure NOT dropping the enrollment, single 401 retry, no infinite retry loop, and the localStorage/sessionStorage write guard.
